  "contentPlaintext": "<bold>RFC-001: Streaming Chunk Parser Implementation</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">RFC Identifier:</textStyle></bold> RFC-2026-001\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Status:</textStyle></bold> Proposed\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Context Date:</textStyle></bold> May 28, 2026\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Proposing Author:</textStyle></bold> [Insert Contributor Name]\n<bold>1. Table of Contents</bold>\n1. Table of Contents\n2. Feature Motivation & Problem Context\n3. Detailed Technical Specification\n3.1 Proposed Architecture Upgrades\n3.2 Breaking Changes & API Variations\n4. Performance & Resource Footprint Impact\n5. Unresolved Gaps & Open Community Questions\n<bold>2. Feature Motivation & Problem Context</bold>\n<bold>2.1 The Container Memory Bottleneck</bold>\nThe core platform library currently loads inbound JSON payloads directly into full-text runtime arrays before executing schema validations. While this mechanism is simple to maintain, it triggers severe memory footprint leaks when handling large batch files near our absolute 25MB boundary limits.\n<bold>2.2 Core Performance Degradation</bold>\nExposing the current engine layout to files over 20MB causes container nodes to exhaust their allocated memory pools. This problem triggers system garbage collection loops that stall all active processing pipelines, pushing P95 latency metrics past our <bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">300-millisecond</textStyle></bold> target ceiling.\n<bold>3. Detailed Technical Specification</bold>\n<bold>3.1 Proposed Architecture Upgrades</bold>\nThis proposal suggests replacing the legacy full-text validation array models with an asynchronous streaming chunk parser architecture:\n[ Raw Network Payload ] ➔ [ Data Chunk Stream ] ➔ [ Pipeline Buffer ] ➔ [ Validation Core ]\nThe system will ingest data payloads in localized 64 Kilobyte chunks, running schema validation rules on individual rows instantly. This design approach prevents the application from buffering large source text strings within host execution memory pools.\n<bold>3.2 Breaking Changes & API Variations</bold>\nTo support streaming inputs, the public ingestion endpoint signature must shift from synchronous payload formats to non-blocking data channels:\n// Legacy Interface Pattern (To Be Deprecated)\nfunction ingestPayload(rawInput: string): IngestionReceipt;\n\n// Proposed Asynchronous Streaming Configuration\nasync function streamPayload(inputStream: ReadableStream): Promise<StreamingReceipt>;\n<bold>4. Performance & Resource Footprint Impact</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Memory Stabilization Performance:</textStyle></bold> Initial benchmark validations prove that streaming data keeps container memory allocations below <bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">150MB</textStyle></bold>, regardless of total incoming package size.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Latency Overhead Optimization:</textStyle></bold> Eliminating full-text array reads minimizes garbage collection pauses, cutting P95 transport latency metrics to <bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">184 milliseconds</textStyle></bold>.\n<bold>5. Unresolved Gaps & Open Community Questions</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Question 1 (Buffer Sizing):</textStyle></bold> Should the 64 Kilobyte chunk threshold map to a hard-coded layout limit, or should it auto-scale based on available hardware cores during initialization?\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Question 2 (Error Reporting):</textStyle></bold> How should the streaming engine surface validation failures to the client interface if a schema corruption error occurs near the end of a deep payload stream?",
